---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Writeup from '../../layouts/Writeup.astro';
import puzzlePieces from '../../images/cnote/puzzle-pieces.jpg';
import signalChain from '../../images/cnote/signal-chain.png';

const links = [
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/Jableman19/CNote' },
  { label: 'Demo video', href: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISGjMeqMMFM' },
  { label: 'Product site', href: 'https://piyawatm.wixsite.com/c-note' },
];
---

<Writeup
  title="C-Note"
  description="An augmented-reality app that teaches music students sound design through physical puzzle pieces — turning abstract audio concepts into hands-on play."
  meta="2023 · AR/VR Capstone · University of Michigan"
  tags={['Unity', 'C#', 'AR', 'DSP']}
  links={links}
>
  <p>
    Sound design is everywhere in the media we consume, but the barriers to entry are steep. An
    entry-level synthesizer costs upwards of $300, and even then, learning to coax a sound out of an
    array of unlabeled buttons and knobs is unintuitive at best — discouraging at worst, especially
    for a kid encountering the subject for the first time.
  </p>
  <p>
    For our AR/VR capstone, our team built <strong>C-Note</strong>: a cheap, simple, and tactile
    sound-design environment aimed at middle-school students. Instead of knobs, students get a set
    of physical puzzle pieces, each representing a way to create or modify sound — an oscillator, a
    filter, an effect.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <Image
      src={puzzlePieces}
      widths={[480, 720, 1080]}
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 94vw, 656px"
      alt="A set of C-Note's colorful physical puzzle pieces laid out on a table, each printed with an audio-effect symbol"
      format="webp"
    />
    <figcaption>The array of audio-effect puzzle pieces.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Snap pieces together into a chain, point an iOS device at it, and the app interprets the chain
    in real time — reading the pieces left to right like a signal path and converting them into a
    live audio signal. Creations can be saved and played back through the
    app's <strong>songbook</strong> feature, so a class can collect and revisit what they've
    built.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <Image
      src={signalChain}
      widths={[480, 720, 1080]}
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 94vw, 656px"
      alt="Diagram showing puzzle pieces composing into an audio signal chain, interpreted left to right"
      format="webp"
    />
    <figcaption>Audio effects are interpreted left to right, like a signal chain.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What it taught me</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Digital signal processing</strong> — building the effect chain meant understanding how
      oscillators, envelopes, and filters actually compose, not just how to patch them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The MIDI system</strong> — mapping physical arrangements to musical events in real
      time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Product design and playtesting</strong> — our target users were middle-schoolers, so
      every interaction had to survive contact with actual kids. Watching a playtest fall apart is a
      faster teacher than any spec review.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The app is built in <strong>Unity (C#)</strong>, using image tracking to recognize the pieces
    and custom shaders for the AR overlay.
    The <a
      class="ilink"
      href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ISGjMeqMMFM"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">demo video</a
    > shows the full loop — assembling a chain, scanning it, and hearing the result.
  </p>

  <p class="credit mono-meta">
    Photos and portions of this write-up adapted from
    teammate <a
      href="https://behrmanz.github.io/projects/7_project/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Zach Behrman's project page</a
    >.
  </p>
</Writeup>

